    description:
      "Bare Ground takes a fundamentally different approach than every granular product on this list: it's a liquid brine designed to be sprayed on concrete before a storm arrives, preventing ice from ever forming a strong bond with the surface in the first place. This anti-icing strategy, borrowed directly from state DOT winter maintenance practices, is more effective and more concrete-friendly than reactive de-icing after the fact. The magnesium chloride base is one of the least aggressive chloride compounds toward concrete because it requires a lower total chloride load per square foot to achieve the same ice-prevention effect as sodium or calcium chloride granules, meaning less total salt contacts your slab over a season. During our side-by-side comparison, driveway sections pre-treated with Bare Ground the evening before a snowfall required little to no shoveling the next morning — the snow simply slid off or was easily broom-swept away because it never formed a bonded ice sheet. Application is straightforward with the included pump sprayer or any garden sprayer: a light, even coating across the concrete surface 12-24 hours before expected precipitation is all that's needed. The liquid also flows into control joints, expansion joints, and hairline cracks that granular product simply sits on top of, providing protection exactly where water tends to pool and refreeze. It performs effectively down to -20°F when used as a pre-treatment, though like all liquid brines it will wash away in heavy rain and need reapplication. At around $20 per gallon covering roughly 1,000-1,500 square feet per application, it's an excellent complement to a granular product rather than a full replacement — spray before the storm, then keep a bag of Safe Paw or a similar concrete-safe granule on hand for any ice that does form.",
