        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Concrete-Safe Ice Melt Matters -->
        <div id="why-concrete-safe-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Concrete-Safe Ice Melt Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete is porous. Under a microscope, it's riddled with tiny capillaries that absorb moisture — and when that moisture contains dissolved chloride salt, the damage mechanism goes far beyond ordinary freeze-thaw cycling. Water alone expands about 9% when it freezes, which is enough stress on its own to slowly degrade an unsealed slab over many winters. But <strong>chloride-based deicers</strong> — rock salt (sodium chloride) and calcium chloride — make the problem dramatically worse through a process called <strong>scaling</strong> or <strong>spalling</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            When chloride salt dissolves in the surface water of your driveway, it lowers the freezing point of that water in the outer layer of the slab while the water deeper in the concrete freezes normally. This creates a sharp pressure differential right at the surface, and repeated cycles of this uneven freezing physically pry apart the top few millimeters of concrete — the exact layer that contains the cream-rich, most weather-resistant part of the pour. Over a season or two of heavy salt use, this shows up as a rough, flaking, pitted surface where the driveway used to be smooth. In more severe cases, chloride ions migrate deep enough to reach embedded rebar or wire mesh, triggering <strong>corrosion</strong> that expands the steel and cracks the concrete from the inside out — a much more expensive problem than surface scaling alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            New concrete is especially vulnerable. A slab continues curing and developing its full density and strength for up to a year after the pour, and its resistance to freeze-thaw damage isn't fully established until that process completes. Most concrete contractors explicitly recommend avoiding deicing salts entirely during a slab's first winter. Stamped, colored, and exposed-aggregate decorative concrete is similarly at risk, since scaling damage is far more visible — and far more expensive to repair — on a finish you paid a premium for. This is exactly why <strong>concrete-safe ice melt</strong> products exist: formulas built around non-chloride chemistry, or chloride blends engineered to minimize surface exposure, that clear ice without triggering the scaling and corrosion that plague traditional rock salt.
          </p>

