        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Ice Melt Chemistry -->
        <div id="types-of-ice-melt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Ice Melt Chemistry: Which One Is Right for Your Driveway?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best ice melt for concrete</strong> depends on your climate, your concrete's age and finish, and whether pets or plants share the treated area. Here are the four chemistries you'll encounter, and how each behaves on a concrete surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rock Salt (Sodium Chloride)</h3>
          <p>
            The cheapest and most widely available option, <strong>sodium chloride</strong> is effective down to roughly 15-20°F but drops off sharply in colder conditions. It's also the chemistry most strongly associated with concrete scaling because it requires a relatively large total volume to do its job, meaning more chloride solution sits against the slab surface through repeated freeze-thaw cycles. Best for: mature, sealed, non-decorative concrete where cost matters more than surface preservation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Calcium Chloride</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Calcium chloride</strong> generates heat through an exothermic reaction on contact with moisture, melting ice faster and at much lower temperatures — down to around -15°F — than rock salt. Products like <strong>Green Gobbler Ice Slicer</strong> use this chemistry. The tradeoff is that its concentrated chloride solution can be just as damaging to concrete, if not more so in the short term, especially on new or unsealed surfaces. Best for: fast-acting relief on mature concrete in genuinely cold climates.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Magnesium Chloride</h3>
          <p>
            Slightly gentler than sodium or calcium chloride, <strong>magnesium chloride</strong> requires a lower total chloride load to achieve the same melting effect, and it's frequently used in liquid pre-treatment brines like <strong>Bare Ground Concrete Safe Liquid Ice Melt</strong>. It remains effective in genuinely extreme cold, down to around -20°F when applied as a brine. Best for: anti-icing pre-treatment before a storm arrives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CMA and Potassium-Based "Concrete Safe" Blends</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Calcium magnesium acetate (CMA)</strong> and potassium-based blends were originally developed to protect bridge decks and highway infrastructure from chloride corrosion, and that same chemistry is now available for residential use in products like <strong>Snow Joe Melt-2-Go Nature+</strong> and <strong>Xynyth Blue Barrel</strong>. Because CMA doesn't ionize into aggressive chloride the way salt does, it is genuinely non-corrosive to concrete and rebar, and it's typically pet and plant safe as well. The tradeoff is slower melting in extreme cold (below about 20°F) and a higher price per pound. Best for: new concrete, decorative finishes, homes with pets, and gardens bordering the treated area.
          </p>

